Subject: [PATCH 71/71] forth/device: create-instance recorded the alloc size BEFORE clearing the head, so destroy-instance always freed 0 -- every instance block leaked

Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc unix

EVERY INSTANCE BLOCK LEAKED, on every arch, since forever. create-instance
(forth/device/device.fs) alloc-mem's an instance block of `>dn.isize` bytes and
saves that size in `>in.alloced-size` so destroy-instance can release it. But it
saved the size FIRST, then `inst-node.size 0 fill`'d the head and copied the node
`itemplate` over the head -- and `>in.alloced-size` is a field INSIDE that head
(offset 1 cell, forth/device/structures.fs). Both the fill and the move
overwrote it back to 0. So destroy-instance read `>in.alloced-size @` = 0 and
`free-mem`'d ZERO bytes: the block was never returned to the alloc-mem free list.

MEASURED, not reasoned (TODO B.4 finding a). A `free-total` probe (sum of the
alloc-mem free list) over one `open-dev`+`close-dev` of `cd:\FILE` on x86: free
memory fell a constant ~352 B per cycle regardless of file size. A per-size
histogram of every alloc-mem and free-mem in the cycle decomposed it exactly: the
six instance blocks (0x2c/0x30/0x30/0x34/0x48/0x58 = 352 B) were allocated and
NEVER freed, while every strdup'd string (args, alias, interpose-args) was. After
this patch the same histogram shows all six sizes in the free log and free-total
is FLAT across repeated loads (0x8d7c7 → 0x8d7c7 → 0x8d7c7).

THE FIX mirrors the two head fields upstream ALREADY sets after the move
(`>in.instance-data`, `>in.my-parent`): move the `>in.alloced-size` store to
after the `0 fill` and the `itemplate` move, so it survives into
destroy-instance. One statement reordered; no behaviour change beyond the block
now being reclaimed.

NOTE: finding (a)'s ORIGINAL characterization ("a buffer sized to the file; ~1.3
MiB exhausts the heap; the next load silently no-ops; a stale image go re-enters")
was a misdiagnosis, corrected in #489 and confirmed here: the leak is a fixed ~44-88
B per instance (~352 B/load), ~1600 loads to exhaust the 578 KiB heap, and does not
produce a no-op in normal use. A separate grubfs C-malloc leak is a no-op on this
lab's doors (arch/{x86,amd64}/lib.c free() is a stub) and is left for a ppc/sparc
pass.

Verified by: ./smoke-openbios.sh elf-ladder (open/close/state-valid/go on all four
doors), multiboot, nvram, unix -- all green with the block now freed.

--- a/forth/device/device.fs
+++ b/forth/device/device.fs
@@ -158,20 +158,26 @@
 : create-instance ( phandle -- ihandle|0 )
   dup >dn.isize @ ['] alloc-mem catch if 2drop 0 exit then
   >r
-  \ we need to save the size in order to be able to release it properly
-  dup >dn.isize @ r@ >in.alloced-size !
 
   \ clear memory (we only need to clear the head; all other data is copied)
   r@ inst-node.size 0 fill
-  
+
   ( phandle R: ihandle )
 
   \ instantiate data
   dup >dn.methods @ r@ instance-init
   dup >dn.priv-methods @ r@ instance-init
 
-  \ instantiate 
+  \ instantiate
   dup >dn.itemplate r@ inst-node.size move
+
+  \ Save the alloc size so destroy-instance can release the block. This MUST
+  \ come AFTER the `0 fill` and the itemplate `move` above: >in.alloced-size
+  \ lies within the inst-node head (offset 1 cell), so the upstream store done
+  \ before them was overwritten -- left at 0 -- and destroy-instance then
+  \ free-mem'd 0 bytes, leaking EVERY instance block (~44-88 B each). Set here
+  \ with >in.instance-data / >in.my-parent, the other head fields written post-move.
+  dup >dn.isize @ r@ >in.alloced-size !
   r@ r@ >in.instance-data !
   my-self r@ >in.my-parent !
   drop
